======================== Eviction policies ========================

LRU
Least recently used removed.
Good general-purpose.

LFU
Least frequently used.
Better for stable hot keys.

TTL eviction
Expired first.

noeviction
Writes fail when memory full.
Critical for reliability-sensitive systems.

======================== Cache invalidation ========================

A. TTL based (Time based)
SET user:1 data EX 300
Easy but stale window exists.
Problem: Figuring out the optimal amount of time.

B. Explicit invalidation (Event based)
On update:
await db.update(...);
await redis.del(`user:${id}`);
Next read reloads fresh value.
Better than long TTLs.
Problem: At every instance where you are updating user data, you have to invalidate cache.

Correct ordering
Usually:
1. update DB
2. delete cache

NOT:
delete cache
update DB

Why?
Because concurrent readers can repopulate old data.

C. Local Caching
Cache stored inside application memory.
const cache = new Map();

Easy and extremely fast.
Problem:
Each server has its own cache.
Server A cache != Server B cache
Cache is also lost after restart/deployment.

D. Distributed Caching (Redis / Memcached)
Centralized cache shared across all servers.
App Servers -> Redis -> Database

Common tools:
Redis
Memcached

Advantages:
Shared cache across instances
Reduces DB load
Better consistency

Problem:
Network latency
Cache invalidation complexity
Extra infrastructure dependency

Redis is more commonly used because it supports:
TTL
Pub/Sub
Distributed locks
Persistence
Advanced data structures

======================== Cache Hit Ratio ========================

Percentage of requests served directly from cache instead of hitting the database.
Cache Hit Ratio = Cache Hits / Total Requests

Depends on:
TTL duration
Cache size
Data access patterns
Cache eviction strategy

Example:
90 cache hits out of 100 requests = 90% hit ratio

======================== Cache stampede ========================

Cache expires.
1000 requests arrive simultaneously.
All hit DB.
DB dies.
-> thundering herd

Solutions
A. Mutex locking
Only one request rebuilds cache.
Others wait/retry.

B. Request coalescing
Merge concurrent requests.

C. Stale-while-revalidate
Serve stale data temporarily.
Background refresh.

D. Jitter TTL
all keys expire at 12:00

ttl = 300 + random(0, 60)
Prevents synchronized expiry.

======================== Hot keys ========================

One key gets huge traffic.

celebrity profile
flash sale product

Problem:
single Redis node bottleneck
uneven load

Solutions:
local in-memory cache
replication
key sharding
request collapsing

